Cell[TextData[{
  "The basic question is how to find the ",
  StyleBox["coordinates",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the point on the vertical plane that corresponds to the the point ",
  StyleBox["{x,y,0}", "InlineInput"],
  " on the horizontal plane.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can solve this by ",
  StyleBox["vector algebra",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", because all points on the vertical plane (point = end of vector) are ",
  StyleBox["orthogonal",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the unit vector ",
  StyleBox["{1,0,0}", "InlineInput"],
  ".  Thus, we just have to invoke ",
  StyleBox["the parametric representation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of points on the line between ",
  StyleBox["{1,1,1}", "InlineInput"],
  "and ",
  StyleBox["{x,y,0}", "InlineInput"],
  " and solve for the value of the parameter that puts the point ",
  StyleBox["on",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the vertical plane.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell["Here it is:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[x, y, z, t]; \n
    sol = Solve[{1, 0, 0} . 
            \(({1, 1, 1} + t\ \(({x, y, 0} - {1, 1, 1})\))\) == 0, {t}]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Next, we substitute the solution back into the representation and \
do some algebraic simplification to get a nice looking answer:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Together[{1, 1, 1} + t\ \(({x, y, 0} - {1, 1, 1})\) /. 
        sol\[LeftDoubleBracket]1\[RightDoubleBracket]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We have gone from points on ",
  StyleBox["one plane",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to points on ",
  StyleBox["another plane",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", because we were using the 3D construction of the projection with the aid \
of projecting lines in space.  But, once we find an algebraic formula, we can \
use this to give \"the same\" transformation from a plane ",
  StyleBox["into itself",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is the definition of the ",
  StyleBox["related",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " transformation as a function on points of the form ",
  StyleBox["{x, y, 1}",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", which consists of points on the plane ",
  StyleBox["z == 1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  PageBreakAbove->True],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(F[{x_, y_, 1}] := {\(y - x\)\/\(1 - x\), \(-\(x\/\(1 - x\)\)\), 1}; 
    \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, suppose we have three points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({a\_1, a\_2, 1}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({b\_1, b\_2, 1}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({c\_1, c\_2, 1}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".  ",
    FontFamily->"Courier"],
  "We",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontFamily->"Courier"],
  "want to know when the ",
  StyleBox["image",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " are collinear.  So we map the points and use ",
  StyleBox["The Determinant Check",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (to be explained later):"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    RowBox[{"Together", "[", 
      RowBox[{"Det", "[", 
        RowBox[{"F", "/@", 
          StyleBox[\({{a\_1, a\_2, 1}, {b\_1, b\_2, 1}, {c\_1, c\_2, 1}}\),
            FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "]"}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
When we compare this with the determinant of the original three \
points, we see that the numerator above is just the negative of the \
determinant.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Det[{{a\_1, a\_2, 1}, {b\_1, b\_2, 1}, {c\_1, c\_2, 1}}]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Thus, we have \"proved\" that under projection (at least in this \
one instance), three points are collinear if, and only if, the image points \
are.\
\>", "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["A problem with central projections ", "Subsubsection",
  CellMargins->{{Inherited, 73}, {Inherited, Inherited}},
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The central projection we just discussed had as its ",
  StyleBox["center",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the point ",
  StyleBox["{1,1,1}", "InlineInput"],
  ".  The only reason for making use of this point was that it was easy \
\[LongDash] for a time \[LongDash] to work with the coordinate planes in \
ordinary ",
  StyleBox["xyz",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-space in order to find a ",
  StyleBox["simple formula",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " for an example of a projective transformation between planes.  "
}], "Text",
  CellMargins->{{Inherited, 73}, {Inherited, Inherited}},
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  "A more ",
  StyleBox["symmetric choice",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of a center is to move the centeral point to the origin ",
  StyleBox["{0,0,0}", "InlineInput"],
  ", since the exact choice of the coordinates of the point clearly makes no \
difference ",
  StyleBox["conceptually",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  CellMargins->{{Inherited, 73}, {Inherited, Inherited}},
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, in a central projection under this convention, the projecting rays \
are ",
  StyleBox["all the lines through the origin",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Given a ray, it intersects each plane (not passing through the origin) \
in a point\[LongDash]unless, of course, the ray is ",
  StyleBox["parallel",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the plane.  This problem of parallelism is just the rub, however:  "
}], "Text",
  CellMargins->{{Inherited, 73}, {Inherited, Inherited}},
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["The Puzzle. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Unless the two planes between which we wish to set up a projective \
correspondence are parallel, the rays ",
  StyleBox["do not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " set up a ",
  StyleBox["one-one correspondence",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " between the points of one plane and the points of the other plane.  "
}], "Text",
  CellMargins->{{Inherited, 73}, {Inherited, Inherited}},
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False,
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell["\<\
This problem can be seen in the following two diagrams (showing, \
actually, central projections between lines).  The first case shows there is \
a ono-one correspondence; the second case shows that there are exceptional \
points on each of the two planes.\
\>", "Text",
  CellMargins->{{Inherited, 73}, {Inherited, Inherited}},
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

